>>
>> I don't undertand what are event loop and how may I use them
>>
>>
> REPL (read-eval-print-loop) is the way console applications usually work.
> They read (wait for input line from the user), evaluate that input (as
> expression), print the result (on the console) and loop over again. In order
> to support interactivity the loop also checks for events from the user
> interface and acts upon them. If you are using JRI you can use R simply by
> evaluating expressions in R in which case you are not using the R event loop
> so R cannot act upon them (e.g. when you create or resize a graphics
> window). You can also use JRI in REPL mode (by passing runMainLoop=true and
> corresponding RMainLoopCallbacks) - this is for example what JGR does.
>
> In general it is not a good idea to use the default interactive R graphics
> devices in JRI, because they use native code that is system-dependent and
> may clash with Java's window management. That's why I'd strongly suggest you
> use JavaGD if you want to use JRI with R graphics.
>
> Cheers,
> Simon
>
